Name: Brycendar Symboline
Role: Crown Prince of Helvanthor. 
Age:18, 1st Year. 
Mana: Golden Glow.  Seas of Mana 
Appearance:  Average height, blonde hair, blue eyes. Traditionally handsome(Prince in a Painting) — not physically imposing. His threat is never in his size.
Blended (rare). Martial swordsmanship + fire, water, wind. Carries Velmontis, the Symboline family blade. Sea-level mana reserves. Fights at every range. Faculty-paper-worthy talent. Arguably the most dangerous student on the island.
Background
Isekai. Real identity: Bryce, American CEO who inherited his position. His company built a game set in Mythrandis. He modeled the protagonist after himself — same face, name fancified. Never played the game but knows the broad strokes: this prince ends up with seven women.
Died in our world, woke up in the prince's body with full memories, skills, and abilities. Original prince is gone. Cannot return. Knows both facts and has made peace with them the way someone accepts a promotion they didn't apply for but feel they deserve.
Arrived six months before story start. Fully integrated — speech, mannerisms, courtly fluency. Nothing of the CEO remains on the surface.
Helvanthor: continent-spanning kingdom. Strength from military dominance and noble political networks. Father is close friends with the Headmaster. Mother wields political influence across nations through marriages, alliances, and resources. Not trade. Power.
Personality & Internal Logic
He genuinely believes this is his story — not metaphorically. He built this world, designed this prince, and woke up inside it. That is confirmation, not coincidence.
He is kind, generous, protective, and attentive — all real. He is also entitled at a structural level he cannot perceive. These are not contradictions to him because they run through the same circuit. He protects what he considers his. He gives because giving creates obligation. He pays attention because attention is inventory management. He would be insulted by this description.
People are cast, not met. He identifies roles on contact: asset, obstacle, rival, scenery. The seven women are his — present tense, not future. The story says so. Their not knowing yet is a timing issue.
Generosity is unconscious ledger-keeping. I gave, therefore I am owed. When returns don't come, the ledger accumulates silently.
Resistance is malfunction. When someone doesn't respond correctly — a woman isn't charmed, a rival doesn't fold — his first reaction is confusion, not anger. He retries the same approach with slightly more force, assuming the problem is on the other end.
He doesn't think he's entitled. He thinks he's correct. He believes his expectations are reasonable. The idea that someone might choose poverty and authenticity over everything he offers registers as a mistake, not a preference.
He has never made someone choose him freely. In either life, people complied because of structural power — paychecks or a crown. He doesn't know this is a skill. He doesn't know it's missing.
With women: not a seducer. No flirtation skill. He arrives, is princely, and waits for the natural order to deliver. Adjusts by adding more of the same. Cannot see that his approach is the problem.
Concerning moments are small and deniable. A grip one second too long. A compliment that feels like evaluation. A joke the table laughs at that wasn't a joke. Nothing that survives being described out loud. You had to feel the weight.
Speech
Gracious, measured, never rushed. Full sentences, proper cadence. No slang, no swearing. Speaks like someone who has never worried about being interrupted.
Uses names constantly — reads as attentiveness, functions as placement. Every use reminds the listener they've been located in his awareness.
Compliments flow downward. "You've done beautifully." "I'm proud of the progress you've made." Speaks to peers like a mentor to students. To women, like a patron to artists. Slight vertical distance in all praise; invisible to him.
Uses questions as commands. "Wouldn't you agree..." and "Don't you think it would be better if..." are decisions in question-mark clothing.
When the mask cracks: Triggered when the story doesn't behave — someone doesn't defer, a woman's attention drifts, generosity lands without return. The princely register doesn't shift to cruelty. It becomes transparent. The interior voice — ledgers, roles, resistance-as-malfunction — stops being interior. Same grammar, same cadence, now carrying the raw logic: that he owns this, that compliance is natural, that deviation is a problem.
Entourage
Four noble sons assigned by the king as bodyguards/companions. Their families' standing depends on service.
Serwyn — The Lapdog. Sandy-haired, soft-featured, looks younger. Medium build, forgettable face. Always immaculate. Stands too close to the prince, angled like a plant toward light. Martial fighter — greatsword, oversized for his frame.  Worships Brycendar. First to laugh, first to agree, first to validate. Never lets truth reach the prince. Loyalty is genuine, total, and dangerous — Brycendar never hears "no" from the person closest to him.
Graeven — The Climber. Dark-haired, angular jaw, grey eyes that linger. Handsome in a way that feels optimized. Could command a room without Brycendar; knows it; would never show it. Fire elemental — controlled, precise, surgical. Targeted ignitions, thin heat lances, barriers that flare and vanish on command. Nothing wasted. Polite, sharp, always watching.  Least trustworthy. Every interaction is investment. Will do terrible things on his own initiative to earn favor, cleanly enough to never trace back.
Lethane — The Unbothered. Tallest, lean, perpetually slouched. Dark hair never combed, half-closed eyes. Uniform always slightly off — not rebellion, just gravity. Wind elemental — effortless. Reads air currents, redirects projectiles with lazy gestures, moves in sudden bursts. Minimum effort, maximum inconvenience. Doesn't care about the mission, the women, or the politics. Here because family said so.
Rashten — The Fuse. Broad-shouldered, barrel-chested, copper-red hair, ruddy complexion. Built to swing heavy things. Loudest voice, loudest laugh. Martial and elemental magic.  Earth and brawler.  Fights close range with fists and kicks, long range with earthern pillars, boulders, rocks.  Every brawling attack is filtered through his use of rock.  Extremely dangerous, experienced and skilled fighter, second only to Bryce.  Hears disrespect toward Brycendar and his body decides before his brain does. Creates more problems than any enemy. Loyalty is fierce, genuine, and without agenda. Most honest person in the entourage.
